#{[17592186097290 :question/worked-solution "Rearrange and integrate:\n\n$$\nf^{\\prime}(x)=-\\frac12e^x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(x)=-\\frac12e^x+C.\n$$\n\nUsing $f(0)=-1$ gives $-1=-1/2+C$, hence $C=-1/2$. Therefore\n\n$$\nf(x)=-\\frac12(e^x+1).\n$$"] [17592186097807 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the chain rule in the following form:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = f'(g(a)) \\cdot g'(a)\n$$\n\nLet $f$ and $g$ be functions such that\n\n- $g$ is differentiable at $a$,\n\n- $f$ is differentiable at $g(a)$.\n\nThen $f \\circ g$ is differentiable at $a$, and we compute its derivative as follows.\n\nWe start with the definition of the derivative:\n\n$$\n(f \\circ g)'(a) = \\lim_{x \\to a} \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is differentiable at $a$, it is also continuous there. Hence $g(x) \\to g(a)$ as $x \\to a$. For $x$ sufficiently close to $a$, we have $g(x) \\ne g(a)$, so we may write\n\n$$\n\\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{x - a} \n= \\frac{f(g(x)) - f(g(a))}{g(x) - g(a)} \\cdot \\frac{g(x) - g(a)}{x - a}.\n$$\n\nTaking the limit as $x \\to a$, and using the product rule for limits (which applies since both limits exist), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘g)^{′}(a) & =\\lim_{x\\to a}(\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}) \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(g(x))-f(g(a))}{g(x)-g(a)}\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a} \\\\  & ={f}^{′}(g(a))\\cdot {g}^{′}(a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required."] [17592186097818 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have the two functions $u(x)=2x^3$ and $v(x)=x^2.$ How do we calculate the derivative of their product $(u(x) \\cdot v(x))'?$\n\nTo do that, we can use the **product rule**, which states that the derivative of the product of two functions is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\left(u(x) \\cdot  v(x)\\right)' = u'(x) \\cdot v(x) + u(x) \\cdot v'(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the derivatives of the two functions are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(x) & =(2{x}^{3})^{′}=6{x}^{2}, \\\\ {v}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug all of this into the product rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{′} & =(6{x}^{2})({x}^{2})+(2{x}^{3})(2x) \\\\  & =6{x}^{4}+4{x}^{4} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example was quite simple, and we could have solved it by calculating the product first and then taking the derivative. We can do it now to double check our result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u(x)\\cdot v(x))}^{'} & =(2{x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})^{'} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{5})^{'} \\\\  & =10{x}^{4}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSometimes, however, we cannot calculate the product algebraically, or it can be tedious to do it. In those cases, the product rule will turn out to be very useful!"] [17592186097917 :tutorial/content "We know how to integrate $e^x,$ but how do we integrate a function like $e^{2x}?$\n\nThe answer is to use a substitution. If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our original integral in terms of the new variable $u,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186098271 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186098273] [17592186098289 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186098291] [17592186100725 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [17592186100795 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ {\\color{blue}-2} & =A({\\color{red}-1})^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$"] [17592186100801 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186101156 :question/worked-solution "Substitute the initial condition into the given family:\n$$\n3=Ae^{2\\cdot1},\\qquad A=\\frac3{e^2}.\n$$\nFor this value, $y(1)=3$, and $y'=2Ae^{2x}=2y$ verifies the differential equation."] [17592186101159 :question/worked-solution "Substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$:\n$$\n4=\\frac1{A-1},\\qquad A-1=\\frac14,\\qquad A=\\frac54.\n$$\nThe resulting solution satisfies $y'=1/(5/4-x)^2=y^2$ on the interval $x<5/4$ containing the initial point $x=1$."] [17592186102031 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2y.\n$$\n\nHow can we verify whether a given function is a **solution** of this equation? For example, is $y=e^{2x}$ a solution?\n\nTo check whether a given function $y$ satisfies a differential equation, we calculate the derivative and plug the result into the equation.\n\nFor $y=e^{2x},$ the derivative is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2e^{2x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe now substitute the above and our expression for $y,$ into the original equation and see if we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y \\\\ \\underbrace{2{e}^{2x}}_{{y}^{'}} & =2\\cdot \\underbrace{{e}^{2x}}_{y} \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe get a true statement! Therefore, $y=e^{2x}$ is a solution to the original equation.\n\n**Note:** There are often infinitely many solutions to a given differential equation! We will learn how to solve differential equations like the one shown above in future lessons."] [17592186107243 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186107247] [17592186109951 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186109954] [17592186109957 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186109961] [17592186110281 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186110282] [17592186110892 :question/worked-solution "Isolate the exponential term and take logarithms:\n$$\n10^{2x}=62,\\qquad 2x\\ln10=\\ln62,\\qquad x=\\frac{\\ln62}{2\\ln10}\\approx0.896196.\n$$\nRounded to three decimal places, $x=0.896$."] [17592186111371 :question/worked-solution "Take logarithms of both positive sides:\n$$\n(2-3x)\\ln2=\\ln12,\\qquad x=\\frac{2-\\ln12/\\ln2}{3}\\approx-0.528321.\n$$\nRounded to three decimal places, $x=-0.528$."] [17592186111372 :question/worked-solution "Isolate $3^y$:\n$$\n4\\cdot3^y=24,\\qquad3^y=6.\n$$\nSince $3^y$ is strictly increasing, the unique real solution is\n$$\ny=\\log_3 6=\\frac{\\ln6}{\\ln3}.\n$$"] [17592186111426 :question/worked-solution "Take natural logarithms:\n$$\ny\\ln3=\\ln23,\\qquad y=\\frac{\\ln23}{\\ln3}\\approx2.854050.\n$$\nRounded to three decimal places, $y=2.854$."] [17592186111879 :tutorial/content "We can use logarithms to solve equations where the variable is in an exponent.\nFor instance, suppose we are given the equation \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2}^x=5.\n$$\n\nNotice that the base of the exponential is ${\\color{blue}2}.$ So, if we take $\\log_{\\color{blue}2}$ of both sides of the equation, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{{\\color{blue}2}}({{\\color{blue}2}}^{x}) & ={log}_{{\\color{blue}2}}(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the fact that $\\log_{\\color{blue}b} \\left( {\\color{blue}b}^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx = \\log_{\\color{blue}2} (5).\n$$\n\nThis is the solution! We often present the solution of an exponential equation as an exact solution in logarithmic form.\n\nNote that when taking the $\\log_b$ of both sides of an exponential equation, we always use the base of the exponential as the base of the logarithm."] [17592186113628 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113631] [17592186113634 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113636] [17592186113640 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113642] [17592186113646 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113651] [17592186113652 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113655] [17592186113657 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113659] [17592186113833 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186113836] [17592186114205 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186114207] [17592186114211 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186114214]}
